#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIs the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$ on the intervals $[-1,2]$ and $[-1,0]?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-1}=+\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is *not* continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[-1,0].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval $[-1,0].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-1,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, of the two intervals, the extreme value theorem is only applicable on $[-1,0].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/66cd2a8b7e88849efc1b8275fd35f763984e4444821a9cd0e0e9060a8f90c956.png)\n\n    \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-2,1]$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $[-4,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-2,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-2,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-4,4]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-4,4]$ because there are discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ both of which lie within $[-4,4].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,1]$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)?$ \n\n- $[0,2]$\n\n- $(0,2)$\n\n- $[-2,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-2,0]$ because this interval contains points where $\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)$ is not defined. In particular, $\\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative values of $x.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0,2]$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd9e9d0f-8e52-5713-82d9-1897135e971e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282512} {:answer/id #uuid "336a4b75-a852-5ddf-b437-d229bda107a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282513} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ecd99d-7289-5e25-89c5-30f5d6322029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282514} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7ecdc0-3f32-5901-a91c-4a2083972cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282515} {:answer/id #uuid "7c27d170-5b2e-5396-8c03-2d89fe075213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f7ecdc0-3f32-5901-a91c-4a2083972cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282515} :answer-field/id #uuid "4323838a-86e5-525c-9bd9-01f08dfba2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f91c1d6c-9d29-5bea-b29c-baac3902ec50" :question/math-academy-id "q-127037" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/e97552a634b4e3ad3604de9d75742af05aa30a18ee92c8440e6c3cedd5d7273a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[0,4]$\n- $[1,6]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,4]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=0.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,6]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,6].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91360d6f-6f19-54fb-9712-34203b5cc76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} {:answer/id #uuid "a0026511-73cd-5270-9d3a-ef3068c65637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282520} {:answer/id #uuid "73ee1e57-b6a4-5a53-9949-ded9a87315a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282521} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9a8730-9eb6-571b-89cd-385d2fda08cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282522} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed455ff-d38f-5100-88b4-a6e76267d946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0026511-73cd-5270-9d3a-ef3068c65637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282520} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8f85264-d7ef-5b96-a2b9-bcbbf885ee96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e727db6-0c71-566f-b828-2be30fdb6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/3c25115cfb7ccde7ec0cbec249d62b037c7fb94be92905b186101ce1fd45a1d8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(-\\infty ,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\infty ,0]$ is closed, but it is not a bounded interval in the form $[a,b].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30197e0d-bbbe-59f6-97c4-2e985ffb5df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/