2186282531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7299179f-72ab-5de2-bba0-a4a1c6aab766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282533} {:answer/id #uuid "28a98a10-f40d-5dbc-81e7-818618bbaffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282534} {:answer/id #uuid "181a575f-4008-535e-9845-ce77fc359616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282535} {:answer/id #uuid "574af9d7-220c-5d5a-9cf3-06e321f155c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282536} {:answer/id #uuid "fffccb3f-04db-5d16-af0b-f2877baad35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fffccb3f-04db-5d16-af0b-f2877baad35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282537} :answer-field/id #uuid "896f7efb-7f4a-5954-ae85-eb1f02de9247" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5fd58899-88f6-5607-8eac-85544634fc2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131066" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f859efa-266e-5802-9074-307c7031aff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175015} {:answer/id #uuid "de447de5-3c01-569c-96eb-3429d1b1ea81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175016} {:answer/id #uuid "106b30c4-8e4d-5a6e-ae77-37fd34703c27" :answer/type {:db/iden