538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9b5207-e12f-55a8-b522-ea9e1363c935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282540} {:answer/id #uuid "435842ee-ebcd-5da1-a7c9-e02b4a89fb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282541} {:answer/id #uuid "31f410d0-d136-594a-afbf-48c3a296457c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282542} {:answer/id #uuid "89278a88-d8cd-5c99-b996-3b2e50385b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282543} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4245d1-ff35-5044-8edc-6465fbaed952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed4245d1-ff35-5044-8edc-6465fbaed952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282544} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e30657-ebc5-5d23-8b46-37c71a515a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f97a27ef-55cd-5290-b60b-237687becd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[0.5,1]$\n- $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7addbe24-71f4-5107-b37c-2cea0338f8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282547} {:answer/id #uuid "be355ad1-02b9-50f3-bd14-a4be7ad273ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282548} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfc242d-70a9-526a-8adf-fc6749fa1e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282549} {:answer/id #uuid "aef48172-bcf7-556a-98f9-91b357bbce30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282550} {:answer/id #uuid "78fcedba-5eeb-5a90-bc2a-acb4a5bb8fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cfc242d-70a9-526a-8adf-fc6749fa1e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282549} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4bfb549-aa84-5f99-ad5a-fbe349bddb54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fce4f987-ba14-51e2-8efa-50e28c96c8d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-48018" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡(x-2)?$  \n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[2,3)$\n- $[3,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $y={log}_{2}⁡(x-2)$ is not defined anywhere in this interval. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[2,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[3,4]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[3,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e41c826-f299-578c-a4b7-bfa008552e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282554} {:answer/id #uuid "b84efe7e-6914-5bd5-8e1f-0802578f2a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282555} {:answer/id #