 conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4dcbcd-33e8-5c82-8e12-1e44306b4c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282561} {:answer/id #uuid "839cc2ac-c921-5f42-89af-bd305402cb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282562} {:answer/id #uuid "0330bf23-fe96-58d2-b81d-db1cfce1fcd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282563} {:answer/id #uuid "c45dd512-f40a-51f5-9c4a-0692e0811afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282564} {:answer/id #uuid "0457cfcb-9d9b-599b-86f5-45ce4a0a4827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0457cfcb-9d9b-599b-86f5-45ce4a0a4827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282565} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f3af35-aac1-5921-9bb8-e4882dbc8d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d1ad50d-3e6e-50d5-9eb2-4c4eef3a4f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73463" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/4de4aa4956b0b70e6e08c9f0188944469270ab19501194ce897e2af9411a0d98.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n- $[0,\\pi ]$\n- $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64bef984-2fa4-5e1f-8859-bc2062cc572c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282568} {:answer/id #uuid "10e6e619-b49a-5f29-b910-eddc841e024c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} {:answer/id #uuid "b26fab90-f511-5974-a17a-737f5617c566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282570} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3bf7f7-ca70-5bff-9747-1e684816dbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282571} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf58035-167f-5a7e-ad40-816ebcff77ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64bef984-2fa4-5e1f-8859-bc2062cc572c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282568} :answer-field/id #uuid "30651cf6-0770-5c05-8345-caaaadfc8ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cb3c5e6-b27a-54b3-bddb-224df5a287c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\n- $[-1,5]$\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[2,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9111de02-be51-5192-bc8a-e3e6f212d612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282575} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c0ad19-281e-53f1-a2bf-74c27b8741b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282576} {:answer/id #uuid "76cb2c2c-25a1-5c41-a55b-ba726ea6c1e3" :answer