}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab7ed026-7b7b-5658-bb75-333a67147a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282582} {:answer/id #uuid "6a62d6da-17a4-5fee-a1bb-773125203952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282583} {:answer/id #uuid "89b34b2f-fcd9-529f-b008-e87f0f6b577f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282584} {:answer/id #uuid "9a44aad5-78b8-5020-a4ed-50ef0fa7b275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282585} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9b8abb-a324-56c4-822f-789f712b46cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b34b2f-fcd9-529f-b008-e87f0f6b577f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282584} :answer-field/id #uuid "58452fa1-c652-520e-a63c-5ea1d0e3e1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32a59c4d-07fa-5f81-babd-37c78492897b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73486" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{x})?$   \n\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$\n- $[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$   is closed, and the function is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2353d28-bdbf-534e-a2eb-4daa3d908863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282589} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0571c4-75bb-5bce-9399-fd37ed18c2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282590} {:answer/id #uuid "304c00f8-87e9-5edb-bd4c-b6c76687ea21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282591} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8df3eb-c747-5caa-969a-e599209140ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282592} {:answer/id #uuid "845c5679-0c16-59dc-8ee5-c0bb99cc8724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845c5679-0c16-59dc-8ee5-c0bb99cc8724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282593} :answer-field/id #uuid "23912385-562e-54a5-8b55-dc33f4c2bf7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "433caa2a-aa03-5646-bb0a-de6a7ed00330" :question/math-academy-id "q-73495" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb7702c1-237e-53f3-aa89-942da52cc8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282596} {:answer/id #uuid "629d753a-dfff-515b-b5c3-5d359a1718bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282597} {:answer/id #uuid "53bb38f6-926a-5169-a3bd-89b0521fb34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282598} {:answer/id #uuid "93505524-6f17-5003-a079-677467d8f77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282599} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae011de-ebfb-5dd9-8698-328b5b89c35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93505524-6f17-5003-a079-677467d8f77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282599} :answer-field/id #uuid "3648e0da-cf6b-5396-ab8f-56481b4d6141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22aa8504-e70c-57c7-a7e8-3515ff9ee1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,3]$\n- $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d14eb47-4056-5aa8-bb4e-034503b19308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282603} {:answer/id #uuid "26760daf-00bb-5e81-a1ed-c39ebbea0601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282604} {:answer/id #uuid "395dd8bf-24c7-5dd0-85bc-9c7a9dce0816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282605} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ff5d18-e1fd-5831-a5bc-b2e886054b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282606} {:answer/id #uuid "51a9b52e-4981-5c09-b378-b87a2cab95f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395dd8bf-24c7-5dd0-85bc-9c7a9dce0816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c930ffaa-1e59-54a6-b426-7c5fa6e87ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc352233-846d-594d-b612-bda9c3885224" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
